WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.099 --> 00:00:10.800
So now that we understand the flow of information of where these blueprints come from and are processed,

2
00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.599
let's go over some core concepts in Unreal Engine which will help you on your journey.

3
00:00:15.599 --> 00:00:23.629
The first thing that we need to consider is that a blueprint is an asset that adds functionality on top of an existing gameplay class.

4
00:00:23.629 --> 00:00:31.329
So again, when we're talking about the parent and child classes, we're always adding more functionality to an existing class.

5
00:00:31.329 --> 00:00:36.530
And we define the content and functionality of a C++ class via the visual studio editing,

6
00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:41.030
whereas in blueprints we can edit it directly in the engine.

7
00:00:41.030 --> 00:00:45.729
So the example that you see on your screen here is all under the hood C++,

8
00:00:45.729 --> 00:00:49.530
but it's just a very visual representation of the information.

9
00:00:49.530 --> 00:00:56.229
So here we have a timeline on the left hand side which we will get to in a few minutes here.

10
00:00:56.229 --> 00:01:00.729
We see that the timeline is updating to some kind of set rotation,

11
00:01:00.729 --> 00:01:05.530
which in this example is a door, so we will open a door.

12
00:01:05.530 --> 00:01:12.230
And the rotation that you give the door is based on this lerp at the bottom which is the green function,

13
00:01:12.230 --> 00:01:19.829
a lerp rotator, which essentially is looking at the A and B value and lerping between the A and B value.

14
00:01:19.829 --> 00:01:29.129
So in this example we're going from a zero rotation in Z to a 90 degree rotation in Z which causes the door to open.

15
00:01:29.129 --> 00:01:33.390
It's just a very nice visual workflow here.

16
00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:42.090
All blueprints are based on C++, so it's just a very visual way of thinking about the C++ that's running under the hood.

17
00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:47.489
The blueprint script visualization method will be persistent in many of the subeditors of UE5.

18
00:01:47.489 --> 00:01:54.090
So as we get deeper into Unreal Engine and as we see that blueprint is this glue that holds together

19
00:01:54.090 --> 00:01:59.689
the Unreal Engine through different interfaces that might be in animation graphs,

20
00:01:59.689 --> 00:02:05.489
it might be in character control graphs, it might be in larger level graphs.

21
00:02:05.489 --> 00:02:10.189
Again, not to worry about the specific context of what I'm referring to here.

22
00:02:10.189 --> 00:02:15.590
However, it's useful to know that blueprints are used in many different areas of Unreal Engine

23
00:02:15.590 --> 00:02:24.240
and it's useful to understand these core components because they are persistent across most of the editor.

24
00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:29.740
The common classes that you'll be looking for here are part of the gameplay framework in Unreal Engine.

25
00:02:29.740 --> 00:02:36.740
So they're how you create players and controllers and game rules in the game.

26
00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:41.939
Creating a new blueprint will require you to pick up a parent to be the base of a new blueprint class.

27
00:02:41.939 --> 00:02:46.639
So when we create a new class, and again we'll go ahead and do this in a couple of minutes here,

28
00:02:46.639 --> 00:02:52.639
but you'll be picking a parent class. When we're talking about things inherited from a previous class,

29
00:02:52.639 --> 00:02:55.840
it's useful to know where that parent class comes from.

30
00:02:55.840 --> 00:03:01.340
So before you create any kind of blueprint, you'll be asked who is your parent class

31
00:03:01.340 --> 00:03:06.139
because that comes with a whole bunch of information that Unreal Engine has provided to you

32
00:03:06.139 --> 00:03:10.039
that you then don't need to recode yourself.

33
00:03:10.039 --> 00:03:15.139
You get a bunch of set default characteristics, which again we can override and manipulate

34
00:03:15.139 --> 00:03:20.139
as we spoke about in the previous slides, but it gives you a really good starting point.

35
00:03:20.139 --> 00:03:27.840
So the most common ones here are actor, pawn, character, player controller, game mode, base,

36
00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.039
an actor component, and a scene component.

37
00:03:30.039 --> 00:03:36.800
And we'll get into these and you'll be more familiar by the end of the course about what does what here.

38
00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.400
So the different types of blueprints, and once we've picked a parent class,

39
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:47.599
we can pick a whole series of different blueprints based on the relative functionality that we need.

40
00:03:48.099 --> 00:03:52.400
You might have level blueprints, which if you're working on a game in a specific level,

41
00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:58.099
you might have some custom scripting, a scripted event or something like this,

42
00:03:58.099 --> 00:03:59.699
that would be a level blueprint.

43
00:03:59.699 --> 00:04:05.199
You've got data only blueprints, which can be enumerators or structures

44
00:04:05.199 --> 00:04:09.939
that you can see on the list on the bottom right-hand of your screen there.

45
00:04:09.939 --> 00:04:14.340
We have interfaces, which we touched upon in a couple of these training videos,

46
00:04:14.340 --> 00:04:18.139
but interfaces are essentially a blueprint communication device.

47
00:04:18.139 --> 00:04:24.139
So it says you can communicate from one blueprint to another via an interface.

48
00:04:24.139 --> 00:04:28.939
And the macro and function libraries, which if anyone's familiar with C++,

49
00:04:28.939 --> 00:04:34.540
you can create macros and functions inside a blueprint that house gameplay code.
